  "body": "Proposal 63cf836644d634d2 expected the grounding check to move when the value changes from inside to outside the span, but it was unrunnable because the kernel path was wrong. The proposer misread the rule: the documented kernel is `conformance.kernel.grounding`, not the internal function `oracle._grounding_kernel`. The rules say: \"conformance.kernel.grounding: The harness's grounding verdict for a value, its cited span and the document, no relaxation, value-in-span checked: the input is one JSON object {\\\"value\\\", \\\"span\\\", \\\"document\\\"}; GROUNDED, SPAN_MISSING, SPAN_NOT_IN_DOCUMENT or VALUE_NOT_IN_SPAN.\" The proposal's kernel field names `open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle._grounding_kernel`, and the execution reports `MINI_OPEN_KERNEL_NOT_FOUND: 'oracle' has no '_grounding_kernel'`. The rule as written and the code do not part company; the proposer used an implementation detail from the source docstring instead of the documented kernel id. Next, the proposer should build a proposal whose kernel field is `conformance.kernel.grounding` and whose input object has the three required fields, then the expectation `moves` can be tested.",
